# fixedbugs, M-selfhost-fixpoint, 2026-08-18 (panel 085 R6's sweep).
#
# SYMPTOM: `x: f64 @ 1_0.5` compiled clean and printed **0.0**. At exit 0, with
# no diagnostic, under every flag this project passes. `1.5_5` printed 0.0 and
# `1_000.000_1` printed 0.0 — a program that says one number and runs another,
# which is the worst class this compiler has. The self-hosted compiler had the
# same input as **exit 2**, since `1_0.5` is not a C literal either.
#
# CAUSE: `ir/exprs.rs` read the literal with `parse::<f64>().unwrap_or(0.0)`,
# under the comment *"the parse cannot fail: the lexer already accepted the
# shape"*. `lexer/number.rs` accepts `_` between any two digits and does so on
# **both sides of the point**; Rust's `f64` parser accepts none. So the premise
# was written as a justification and falsified by the shortest program that uses
# the separator the spec grants, while `unwrap_or` turned the failure into a
# number. CLAUDE.md §11's shape twice over: a premise about the world, and a
# fallback pointing in the quiet direction.
#
# FIX: the separators come off before the parse, and the fallback is LOUD. After
# they are gone every shape the lexer can produce is `digits[.digits]`, which
# Rust parses and an out-of-range literal makes an infinity rather than an
# error — so a failure there is the compiler's bug and now says so instead of
# inventing a value. The port needed the other half of the same fact: it emits
# hex from `atof`, which stops at a separator, so `emit_literal.hero` strips
# them too and the two compilers agree byte for byte.
#
# The four rows below are the four the defect covered: a separator left of the
# point, right of it, on both sides, and none at all.

function main()
    left: f64 @ 1_0.5
    right: f64 @ 1.5_5
    both: f64 @ 1_000.000_1
    plain: f64 @ 10.5
    print(left)
    print(right)
    print(both)
    print(plain)
    print(left == plain)
